<section class="phero"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/ceiling-water.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/ceiling-water-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/ceiling-water.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><a href="/appraisal-services-nebraska/">Services</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Water Damage Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal service</p><h1>Water Damage Claim Appraisal for Nebraska Properties</h1><p class="lede">A water loss can look small on day one and very different after the baseboards come off. We value the full extent of the damage, from mitigation through the last coat of paint.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn line-light" href="/free-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Common sources</dt><dd>Pipes, appliances, roofs</dd></div><div><dt>Big issues</dt><dd>Flooring &amp; matching</dd></div><div><dt>Note</dt><dd>Flood is usually excluded</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What property policies usually cover</h2>
<p>Most homeowners and commercial forms cover sudden and accidental discharge of water from plumbing, heating and appliance systems, and water that enters through storm damage to the roof or walls. They usually exclude flood, surface water, long-term seepage and wear. Sorting covered from excluded water is the first question, and that one is about coverage, not appraisal.</p>
<p>Flooding along the Platte, Elkhorn, Loup and other rivers in March 2019 was a reminder that river and surface water are generally a matter for flood insurance, not the homeowners policy.</p>
<h2>Where water claims are underpaid</h2>
<ul>
<li>Drying and mitigation invoices reduced without explanation</li>
<li>Flooring replaced in one room when it runs continuously through several</li>
<li>Cabinets, countertops and built-ins treated as repairable when they are not</li>
<li>Insulation, subfloor and framing behind finished surfaces left out</li>
<li>Contents damaged by water or by the mitigation process</li>
</ul>
<h2>Finished basements</h2>
<p>Finished basements are common in Nebraska homes, and they are where many water claims become expensive. Carpet, pad, drywall, trim and egress window wells all add up. Some losses involve sump pump or drain backup, which is usually covered only by an endorsement with its own limit.</p>
<h2>Documentation that holds up</h2>
<p>Moisture readings, drying logs, photos taken before demolition and invoices from the mitigation company carry real weight on a panel. We gather them early and use them to support the scope.</p>

<h2>Questions about this service</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is a sump pump failure covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually only if you bought a water backup or sump overflow endorsement, and that coverage often has a separate, lower limit.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why did my carrier cut the drying bill?</summary><div class="a"><p>Carriers sometimes apply their own pricing guidelines to mitigation. Drying logs and moisture maps help show the work was necessary.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal decide whether my water damage is covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Appraisal values a covered loss. Whether a type of water is covered is a coverage question for the carrier, and if needed, a court.</p></div></details></div>
